WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.630
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.550
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.180
Hello, everybody.

9
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.600
Hello, everybody.

10
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.200
We have some news about Angela's note cards.

11
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.060
Well, I ran out of note cards last night.

12
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.260
She ran out of note cards, everyone.

13
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.480
I ran out of note cards because that's how many note cards I've been using on this podcaSt.

14
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.680
So I started writing on a photo paper.

15
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.000
Oh, I see that.

16
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:46.820
You kind of cut some photo paper into the size of note cards.

17
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.020
Who do you think I am?

18
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:48.860
I didn't cut it.

19
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.440
Oh, that's for a 4x6 photo.

20
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:51.820
What do you think?

21
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.080
I was up late last night.

22
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:53.400
Oh, my gosh.

23
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.120
They're glossy.

24
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.380
On one side.

25
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.760
And I wrote on the matte side because I wrote on the glossy side.

26
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.320
And I got Sharpie all over me.

27
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.340
Anyways, very exciting, guys.

28
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.720
I have to go buy some note cards today.

29
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:07.340
I have to go to, like, Staples or somewhere.

30
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.620
Staples was Dunder Mifflin's competitor.

31
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:10.860
Yes.

32
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:11.460
Yeah.

33
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.000
And we got a lot of fans write in and tell us about all of the Staples merchandise that

34
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.700
they see scattered around our set.

35
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:18.960
Yeah.

36
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.780
Somebody got really, like, in a tizzy about it.

37
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.240
Do you remember?

38
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:22.840
Oh, yes.

39
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.880
Very upset about some Staples thumbtacks on Pam's desk, I guess.

40
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.360
What is she doing?

41
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.060
All right.

42
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.640
Today, everyone, we are going to talk about The Fire, season two, episode three, written

43
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.180
by B.J.

44
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.480
Novak and directed by Ken Kwapis.

45
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.040
Yes.

46
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.320
Here is my first fast fact.

47
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:42.220
It's very short.

48
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.160
This is the third episode from the team of B.J.

49
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.580
Novak and Ken Kwapis.

50
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:47.920
Mm-hmm.

51
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.460
They teamed up on Diversity Day, they teamed up on Sexual Harassment, and now here they

52
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.640
are again, The Fire, finally tackling some rather non-controversial topics.

53
00:01:59.040 --> 00:01:59.360
Yes.

54
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.180
Wait, does it—do I look like I'm looking at you oddly?

55
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.060
Do I look weird looking at you?

56
00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:05.800
What?

57
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.180
Well, I'm wearing my readers so I can see my note cards.

58
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:10.640
Okay.

59
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:15.520
But I can't see you that well, and I wonder, because you look blurry, do I look weird

60
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:16.900
looking at you or do I look normal?

61
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.720
You aren't blinking a lot, but besides that, you look normal.

62
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.400
Oh, you guys, I got readers.

63
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:24.180
Okay, sorry.

64
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.060
Just put them down at the end of your nose.

65
00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:29.820
You have to do that thing where you look over the top of them at me, like that.

66
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:30.320
Okay.

67
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.660
Now she's got it, guys.

68
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:32.520
Okay.

69
00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:34.460
That was it on the first fast fact.

70
00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:35.700
I mean, I said it was short.

71
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:36.380
That was it.

72
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:37.640
I think that's a great fast fact.

73
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:38.040
B.J.

74
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.780
and Ken made a phenomenal team and created some really, really great episodes.

75
00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:42.460
And you know what?

76
00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:43.760
They're never going to team up again.

77
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.160
That's it.

78
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.160
This is it, guys.

79
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:47.840
Why would you do that?

80
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:48.620
Third and final.

81
00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.440
Oh, you were like, they were so great.

82
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:50.740
Yeah.

83
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:51.660
And never again.

84
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:52.760
And then never again.

85
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:54.780
Here is fast fact number two.

86
00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.040
This was the first episode we shot when we came back from our season one hiatus.

87
00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:00.420
Yeah.

88
00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.720
It was the fire.

89
00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.320
And I have a fan question.

90
00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:04.520
Latamania.

91
00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.240
I hope I said that right.

92
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:07.560
Latamania.

93
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.160
Latamania asks, what was the atmosphere like on set?

94
00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:15.360
What were some of the biggest changes you noticed between filming season one and season

95
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:15.760
two?

96
00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.140
I thought that was a good question.

97
00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.020
It is a good question.

98
00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.740
The biggest difference was that we were no longer shooting in a real office in Culver

99
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:21.960
City.

100
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:28.500
We were now finally at our home on the sound stages deep, deep in the San Fernando Valley

101
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.360
off of Sadekoy Street.

102
00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:35.200
So a lot of TV shows are shot on these big studio lots.

103
00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:36.660
Like, you know, if you come to L.A.

104
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.180
and you get like a tour of Paramount Studios or Universal,

105
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:40.960
that is not where we were.

106
00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:41.980
That's not where we worked.

107
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.060
Those studio lots, they have little like.

108
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:47.300
Cafeterias.

109
00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:47.820
Yeah.

110
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:48.780
Gift shops.

111
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.680
What do you call those places?

112
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:51.260
GyMs.

113
00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:52.240
A gym.

114
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:53.000
I don't know.

115
00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.760
Wait, I don't go often, so I'm not quite sure what they're called.

116
00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.040
We're like, what are those things called?

117
00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.480
What are those places with the muscles and the heavy things?

118
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.780
And you walk in place for a long time.

119
00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:03.420
A gym.

120
00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:04.320
Yes.

121
00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.880
They have gyMs.

122
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.820
And oh, the Warner Brothers has its own preschool.

123
00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:09.520
What?

124
00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:10.740
For the people who work there.

125
00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:13.620
Yeah, you can bring your kids and they go to preschool on the lot.

126
00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.180
There are all these bells and whistles when you work on a lot.

127
00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.260
We did not.

128
00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.620
We worked in this very industrial area.

129
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.080
And on a dead end street, there was definitely no coffee shop.

130
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.120
There was no gym.

131
00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:26.740
There was definitely no gym.

132
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.180
We were across from a train track.

133
00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:29.740
Yep.

134
00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:31.400
And was there like a junkyard?

135
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.320
Yeah, it was like a car scrap towing yard.

136
00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:41.260
And so we would see people in the mornings, very angry, come to claim their towed car.

137
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.380
And if they didn't come, they would sell them for scrap.

138
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.060
There was like usually a line of just really ticked off people.

139
00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:52.680
Oh, and then on my commute, I remember like right before I would turn left to go on our street,

140
00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:57.760
I would sit at this red light and there was a mortuary like where you were going to be cremated.

141
00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:58.180
Yeah.

142
00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:59.500
That was my morning commute.

143
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.420
It was like, well, it was very industrial.

144
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:09.880
The crazy thing is that during the break, before we came back to shoot, I got a guest job on that 70s show.

145
00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.480
Oh, and they were fancy.

146
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.260
Fancy doesn't even begin to describe it.

147
00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:14.580
Really?

148
00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:15.540
Give me the dish.

149
00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:16.040
All right.

150
00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:17.580
So they were on a lot.

151
00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.840
They had a coffee shop that served Starbucks coffee.

152
00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:20.420
Wow.

153
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:20.840
Yeah.

154
00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:21.620
They had a gym.

155
00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:22.900
They had a cafeteria.

156
00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:23.880
Wait.

157
00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.880
Wait, a cafeteria like with a train?

158
00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.300
You walked down a lot like a buffet?

159
00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:27.760
Yes.

160
00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:28.700
You would have loved it.

161
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:29.940
Like a Vegas buffet?

162
00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:30.980
Like a Vegas buffet.

163
00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:31.760
I love a buffet.

164
00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.880
It was very fancy.

165
00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:33.960
That's what I'm telling you.

166
00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.280
What was your character?

167
00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:38.100
I played this character, Stacey Wanamaker.

168
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:46.260
And I was a wedding planner, and I was there to plan the wedding of Wilma Valderrama and Mila Kunis.

169
00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.920
They came and consulted me, and I only worked with the two of them.

170
00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.040
All of my scenes were just with the two of them.

171
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:53.800
Here's the thing.

172
00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.460
That entire cast was so famous.

173
00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:57.920
Ashton Kutcher.

174
00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:58.460
Yeah.

175
00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.240
He was married to Demi Moore at the time.

176
00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:01.840
No.

177
00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:02.420
Yeah.

178
00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.060
Wilma Valderrama was dating Lindsay Lohan at the time.

179
00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:07.000
Oh, my God.

180
00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:07.540
Right?

181
00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:08.320
Topher Grace.

182
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.200
All these guys are there.

183
00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:14.280
And they're all talking about their very famous lives.

184
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.740
And I'm just listening to stories about Lindsay Lohan.

185
00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:20.120
They're talking about it like—

186
00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.120
It's their life.

187
00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:21.800
It's their life.

188
00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:26.960
And I wanted to be like, you guys, I'm just like a regular person, and I'm hearing a lot of dish.

189
00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:32.260
But I have never repeated what I heard because I believe that there is a code of silence when you're on a set.

190
00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:36.280
This is true because I'm looking at her right now like, what the heck, lady?

191
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:37.160
You've held out.

192
00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.900
I don't repeat what I hear on sets.

193
00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.000
I believe that's actually—

194
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Not even to your BFF.

195
00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:43.240
Not even to my BFF.

196
00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:44.220
I'm applauding you.

197
00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:45.240
Hey, thank you.

198
00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:46.640
I don't know that I can say the same.

199
00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:47.120
Oh.

200
00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.180
I think I would tell you.

201
00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.340
I would tell you.

202
00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:50.200
Well—

203
00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.640
You're the vault if someone tells you something.

204
00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:56.760
Just if I overhear stuff like that, I just think that's an actor's space.

205
00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.380
Those people should be able to come to work and talk about their lives and not worry about being in the tabloids.

206
00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:02.240
I just think that's important.

207
00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.840
I think that's important, too.

208
00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.800
But I think you could tell your best friend.

209
00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:07.560
Okay, maybe so.

210
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.000
I wouldn't call a tabloid, but I'd maybe say to you, okay, so guess what?

211
00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.020
Lindsay Lohan.

212
00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:14.240
Well—

213
00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.020
But you know what?

214
00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:15.660
Bless her heart.

215
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:15.940
Okay.

216
00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.560
But I'll also say, these guys were huge stars, and they were so nice.

217
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:26.420
At the end of their taping, they went out into the audience for probably almost an hour and signed autographs.

218
00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:29.420
Like, they had been on the air for something like seven years at this point.

219
00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:30.080
That's really nice.

220
00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:30.780
That's classy.

221
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.180
And I just thought—I thought they were a really classy group.

222
00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.400
I actually met Wilmer a while back.

223
00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:38.980
I do a charity in Kansas for Children's Mercy Hospital.

224
00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:39.960
I love it so much.

225
00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:49.200
And really, the people that started it are Eric Stone Street and Jason Sudeikis, Paul Rudd, Rob Riggle, who was on an episode of The Office later on Booze Cruise,

226
00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:50.760
and Dave Koechner.

227
00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:55.560
They're all local Kansas City boys, and they support Children's Mercy Hospital, which is amazing.

228
00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.900
Because Children's Mercy Hospital takes all kids.

229
00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.660
You don't have to have insurance.

230
00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:01.300
They take you no matter what.

231
00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:01.980
That's amazing.

232
00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:02.560
It's amazing.

233
00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.960
They do a few celebrity things to raise money, and they had a celebrity wiffle ball game.

234
00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:09.900
Is it wiffle ball?

235
00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:10.960
It's wiffle ball.

236
00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:12.240
Like a dog thinks it's a wiffle ball.

237
00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:12.900
Wiffle.

238
00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:13.500
Isn't it wiffle ball?

239
00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:14.520
Wiffle ball.

240
00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:14.620
Oh, God.

241
00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:17.220
I've been calling it wiffle ball for a long time.

242
00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:18.060
Ooh, Ange.

243
00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:26.650
Maybe that's why people do it without wiffle ball.

244
00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:28.130
It's spelled with an I.

245
00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:29.870
W-I-F-F-L-E.

246
00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:32.670
Are you writing wiffle ball?

247
00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:33.290
Wiffle ball.

248
00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:34.190
Oh, good God.

249
00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:34.610
Okay.

250
00:08:34.790 --> 00:08:41.190
So we're playing wiffle ball in Kansas City to raise money for Children's Mercy Hospital.

251
00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.070
And I was on Rob Riggle's team, and he was like,

252
00:08:44.130 --> 00:08:45.930
Kinsey, you cannot strike out.

253
00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:47.650
Jason Sudeikis is pitching.

254
00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:51.730
He's going to throw a lot of wacky stuff at you because it's a wiffle ball.

255
00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:54.010
And he was like, you can't strike out.

256
00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:54.910
I was like, okay, Riggle.

257
00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:57.230
There was like a whole stadium full of people.

258
00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:00.450
Like we played on the practice field for the Kansas City Royals.

259
00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:02.110
Yeah, I've seen it.

260
00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:02.630
It's amazing.

261
00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:03.170
It's a bunch of people.

262
00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.930
And so Wilmer had a bat before me.

263
00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:06.410
Okay.

264
00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:11.150
